todo: what you mean to do, as a tree, in the order you do it. it is the
entry point: you start at its top, and it drives every other todo, for a
project's own todo is one of its nodes. it is the program, and you are
the machine: do what is next, type its state, and next moves on.

  todo/                                the top, where you start
    01-app -> ../app                   a project, linked whole
      todo/01-login                1   next: the first not done
      todo/02-signup               2
    02-taxes                       3   a file: one item
    03-move/                           an item that grew
      README                           its text
      01-boxes                     4
      02-van                       5
    04-invoice -> ../shop/todo/03-invoice
                                   6   one item, of a list not yours

  the walk is 1 to 6: depth first, in the order of the names
  next is the first leaf of the walk neither done nor blocked

the grammar
  node      a file, or a folder; a folder's text is its README, and its
            files with no number are the explorer's, not the todo's
  head      title:, state:, after:, on top of the text
  order     the NN- of a name, in its own list: 01 is done first. it is
            only order: 04-invoice is the node invoice
  children  a folder's numbered entries, as tangle reads a folder; a
            project's, whose own files are not the todo's, its todo/
  link      a symlink is a node that lives elsewhere: a link to a folder
            brings its whole list, a link to a file the one item
  step      a line "- [ ]" or "- [x]": counted on its card, not a node
  id        the name without its NN-, and the path down from the top:
            app/login, which on the page is todo.html#app/login

state: now open fail blocked todo done
  typed at rest, derived in motion
    a note's state is typed: a person moves it by editing
    a node with nodes and no state of its own takes the first of those
    its nodes hold, and its card counts them: 1 of 6 done · 2 open
    a link to a page takes its state from the page's own nodes
    a run's state is derived: ls is the status
  open is a decision for a human, at every level.

each list is closed
  a list orders and names only its own entries: a number is a place in
  its own list, and after: names siblings. so a list reads the same alone
  and linked into another, which is what makes lists compose.
  to do a thing first, make it 01 where it lives, and its list 01 in the
  list above. do not point at it from the top: a pointer is a second
  place for one item, and the two drift. link one item only when the rest
  of its list is not yours.
  lists join by link, not by a scan: a scan finds every folder with a
  todo, but it has no order, and the order is the program.

where ideas come in
  at the top, as a bare name, and they grow in place: a name, a line, a
  README, a folder, a folder with its own todo. the node is the same at
  every size; only the tally changes. when it is work, its text moves to
  its project and the entry becomes a link.

the studio, on its own data (`design/studio`)
  thing     a node is a file or a folder
  lens      the page and the walk, made from the folders. nothing reads
            them back; a page is read only where a link names one
  id        the path down joins the page, the walk and the files
  shelf     the page reads. a card folded shows the tally; open, it holds
            the project's nodes, and j k walk them as the walk does: into
            a node's nodes where it has them, and off its end, folded, on
  terminal  ed types a state, a rename reorders, and todo remakes
  todo and pipeline are one structure with two evaluators, you or the
  runner; the tree and the views do not differ. a gate is an open node,
  a stage's reads are after:. what else the studio can take from it,
  still open: `todo/03-studio/06-entry`.

the verb: todo (`sh/todo`), with `medium/page/todo` making the views
  todo        the walk of the todo you are on, a node a line
  todo -w     its page, todo.html beside the folder, then each page
              under it that is there already
  todo -c     the same, checked by `medium/page/run.mjs`
  it takes its noun from c, and finds the nearest todo at or above it:
  todo at the top is all of it, and todo on a project or an item is
  that part.
